A||||2||William Federle||closed loop mode in a 1984 iron duke||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||02-06-2000||07:58 PM||||Some time this past year there was a thread in which someone said that the iron duke could go into closed loop mode early under some circumstances. I just can't remember what the reason was. My Fiero does this. Does anyone have any idea what could cause this?||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||02-06-2000||09:01 PM||||Closed loop conditions are coolant temp above a certain temp (120F, but varies depending on the calibaration) and the O2 is swing back and forth across .456V. At that point close loop will be enabled. <P>Going in "too soon" could be caused by a bad coolant temp sender. Hard to get the O2 to start swing unless there is tons of noise. That is unlikely.<P>What makes you think it's going closed loop to early?<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000002||William Federle||02-06-2000||09:22 PM||||I'm still not finished finding the cause of a fast idle when the engine is cold, so to avoid the situation of having it idle at 3000 RPM or more at startup, I've connected a switch to terminals A and B and I turn the switch on if I'm just idling (not driving) before the car warms up. It flashes at a fast rate when it's in open loop mode, and at about a 1 hz rate in closed loop mode. I started it up today and it wasn't warm yet and it went into closed loop mode almost right away. It has happened before. <P>I saw a posting this last year in which someone said that some sensor being defective will make it go into closed loop mode almost right away after startup. I've searched for the posting, but haven't found it so far.<BR>||||reg||1
